---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { pageTitle, breadcrumbJsonLd } from '../lib/seo';
---

<BaseLayout
  title={pageTitle('Editorial Policy')}
  description="Where listings come from, the role of AI, and how corrections and removals work."
  canonicalPath="/editorial-policy/"
  jsonLd={breadcrumbJsonLd([{ name: 'Editorial Policy', path: '/editorial-policy/' }])}
>
  <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6">
    <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 sm:text-3xl">Editorial Policy</h1>

    <div class="prose-static mt-6">
      <h2>Where listings come from</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Public discovery.</strong> Automated discovery searches publicly
          indexed sources for community invite URLs. Only public information is used.</li>
        <li><strong>Submissions.</strong> Anyone can submit a public community via the
          submit form. Submissions are reviewed manually before publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Manual curation.</strong> Maintainers may add listings directly
          with documented sources.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The role of AI</h2>
      <p>
        AI (Gemini) helps generate discovery queries, classify candidate listings,
        suggest categories/tags and summarize public evidence. AI cannot independently
        establish truth. Every candidate must be backed by a real URL, and factual
        fields (member counts, dates, sources) require real sources when possible.
        AI never invents URLs, metrics, reviews or endorsements, and it never decides
        what gets published — new discoveries go to a pending queue for human review.
      </p>

      <h2>What gets published</h2>
      <p>
        A listing is published when it has a real public invite URL and passes
        moderation. “Passing moderation” means no obvious harmful content — it is not
        a safety certification. Listings may carry <code>safetyFlags</code> (e.g.
        potential-risk-language) that are shown to readers without us declaring the
        community a scam.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections are accepted via the report form or contact page. We review them
        against evidence and update listings when justified. We do not publish
        unverified corrections as fact.
      </p>

      <h2>Removal / deletion</h2>
      <p>
        Listings can be removed for: confirmed illegality, clear fraud, spam, or at
        the verified request of a community owner (owner-confirmed). Removal requests
        are handled by a human. We prefer marking listings unavailable over silently
        deleting indexed URLs — dead or removed listings keep their detail page with
        an explanatory note where useful, then can be archived later.
      </p>

      <h2>Dead-link handling</h2>
      <p>
        Repeated strong failures move a link to <code>dead</code>. We don't delete a
        listing after one failed request, and bot-blocking is never treated as
        evidence of death.
      </p>

      <h2>Content copying</h2>
      <p>
        We do not republish full third-party content. Descriptions are concise,
        factual summaries based on public evidence.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising and sponsors</h2>
      <p>
        If advertising or sponsored listings are introduced later, they will be
        clearly labeled and never prioritized by payment in the organic listings.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
